\f0\fs22 \cf2 Johnny learned that an ex-Fridge Force operative would be joining Project Kebab the following week, so he moved up a conference with hand-picked thought leaders\'97\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
a cryptologist, an Egyptologist, a mathematician, an art conservator and several varieties of physicist\'97\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 to Friday. Neither the invited experts nor Dr Ortolan made a fuss about it, and Johnny hadn\'92t put the day on Sarah\'92s calendar yet, so she wouldn\'92t notice the move.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$Scr_Ps::0>
\f0\fs22 He booked the largest conference room in Sublime Point\'92s office park which sported views south over the Jamison Valley, ordered the premium catering and put it all on Sarah\'92s tab. There\'92d be an intimate cocktail party after, which he expected Sarah either to decline entirely or drink nothing but water, while the rest of them drank from a premium bar. At somewhere between four and fourteen months\'92 pregnant, Johnny was sure she\'92d probably decline the extravagant hors d'oeuvres as well. With any luck, she might feel faint, or at least have an acute episode of baby brain.\
Ortolan showed up with his senior technical officer. Vilnius Cambridge had been instructed to make sure everyone could connect to the display, which occupied him until Johnny complained they were waiting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Thank you for coming,\'94 Johnny began. \'93Some of you will be familiar with my work.\'94\
\'93Your lab explosion?\'94 the astrophysicist asked.\
\'93The research.\'94\
\'93Your invitation included a photograph of the explosion,\'94 a different kind of physicist remarked.\
Johnny smiled and pat the table. \'93The team responsible have already been fired. Shall we move on?\'94\
There was assent, the pouring of water and the unwrapping of mints. They were the best mints in the catering package.\
\'93Dr Ortolan, if you would be so kind,\'94 Johnny said while he admired the landscape.\
Ortolan gestured over his tablet, and an excessive number of screens on the walls lit up, each the size that usually required customers to buy sufficient popcorn and choc-tops to power up.\
After the welcome slide, Ortolan\'92s slide show cut to an image of the damaged laboratory. Across the wall, the scorched pattern curled into itself.\
\'93God, tax it,\'94 Johnny said through clenched teeth. \'93Everyone has seen the explosion.\'94\
Sarah pressed her palm against her stomach.\
\'93The matter is the patterns we see, not the manner in which they were constructed\'85 or perpetrated as the case may be. For example, this portion of a Jackson Pollock\'97\'94 The slideshow transitioned  as a starburst, not unlike an explosion.  \'93\'97viewed under infrared, produces a ninety per cent match.\'94\
The art conservator leaned forward. \'93Which painting?\'94\
Johnny checked the caption. \'93Does it matter?\'94\
\'93Yes. And how big a portion? A top half of something the size of a greeting card? Or a postage stamp?\'94\
\'93Vilnius, send her the details,\'94 Ortolan instructed his senior technician, who he always called Vilnius, and somehow conveyed that was his surname\'97perhaps addressing him as Cambridge afforded him too much academic credibility.\
The slideshow advanced. Ortolan provided the commentary. \'93Rinehart\'92s Caboose, in the Hancock galaxy. Eighty per cent match to the exp\'97energy phenomena.\'94\
\'93Depending on where you\'92re standing,\'94 the astrophysicist said.\
\'93Earth,\'94 Johnny answered. \'93We\'92re standing on earth.\'94\
The next image showed a carved stone. \'93An icon from an ancient civilisation in the Equatorial British Empire,\'94 Ortolan continued. \'93Associated with knowledge and destruction. Eighty-five per cent. Viewed at twenty-three degrees, it becomes ninety-six.\'94\
\'93Why twenty-three?\'94 Sarah asked.\
\'93The closest match.\'94\
\'93Did you try every angle?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
The mathematician sat back. \'93Then you\'92ve found an angle.\'94\
\'93An exceptionally good one,\'94 Johnny said.\
The Egyptologist enlarged the carving on her tablet. \'93Have you contacted whoever excavated this?\'94\
\'93They\'92re dead,\'94 Ortolan said.\
\'93The institution, then.\'94\
\'93Underwater.\'94\
\'93The collection?\'94\
\'93Privately held.\'94\
The Egyptologist nodded. \'93Of course it is.\'94\
Sarah\'92s fingers tightened against her belly. The slideshow moved on. \
\'93This one requires no adjustment of perspective. This one is a ninety-eight per cent match to a section of a CMB B-mode polarisation map.\'94\
The astrophysicist leaned towards the screen.\
\'93A pattern in the polarisation of the universe\'92s oldest observable light,\'94 Ortolan said. \'93Gravitational waves, we\'92re hypothesising.\'94\
Vilnius swiped on his tablet. The polarisation map slid to the left and made room for the reappearance of the pattern scorched into the rock wall of the trashed lab. After a few seconds, the right frame zoomed into the rock image. Short, fuzzy strokes became easier to see.\
\'93These markings may appear random,\'94 Ortolan said. \'93But analysis detects information
\f2\fs24 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 .
\f0\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0 \'94 The image zoomed in. Ortolan pointed. \'93You see here? Thousands of fine grooves.\'94\
\'93Like an old record,\'94 the cryptologist said.\
\'93As in, a vinyl record?\'94 Sarah asked.\
\'93Yes.\'94\
Johnny leaned forward. \'93We think the oscillations represent digital signals. Coded messages.\'94\
\'93Have you reconstructed one?\'94 the cryptologist asked.\
\'93Not yet,\'94 Ortolan said.\
\'93There are hundreds,\'94 Johnny added.\
\'93Hundreds of what?\'94\
\'93That,\'94 Sarah said, \'93is what we\'92re here to find out.\'94\
\'93Messages from God,\'94 Johnny said.\
\'93Which one?\'94 the Egyptologist asked.\
\'93The original.\'94\
\'93Monotheists always say that. It would help if we knew what they said.\'94\
\'93Which is why I\'92ve brought you all here,\'94 Johnny replied.\
\'93Could it be a musical scale?\'94 Vilnius asked.\
\'93We\'92ve tried that.\'94\
\'93What about a logarithmic scale?\'94\
\'93Give the music a rest, Vilnius.\'94\
A physicist drew a finger along one of the enlarged traces. \'93These look like chromatographic peaks.\'94\
Another physicist nodded. The astrophysicist looked unconvinced.\
Ortolan turned to Vilnius. \'93Send them the data.\'94\
The Egyptologist put down her tablet. \'93We need a corroborating text.\'94\
\'93Explain,\'94 the art conservator asked.\
\'93The same message in a form we can already understand. The Rosetta Stone\'97\'94 the Egyptologist checked her own Egypt-splaining. \'93You understand the difficulty.\'94\
\'93Fine.\'94 Johnny stared at her. \'93Vilnius, order a Rosetta Stone. Express delivery.\'94\
\'93Can we stop calling it a cipher until we know it is one?\'94 the cryptologist asked.\
Johnny\'92s jaw tightened. \'93What would you call it?\'94\
\'93Unidentified information,\'94 the astrophysicist said.\
Ortolan checked his watch, and vaguely recalled who he inherited it from. \'93I have something.\'94\
Johnny waved him on.\
\'93We\'92ve taken more high-resolution images of the grooves, but no degree of magnification helps with decoding them.\'94\
Vilnius brought up a new image. The markings had sharpened. The room quietened.\
\'93What did you do?\'94 Sarah asked.\
\'93Applied a correction to the images,\'94 Vilnius answered.\
Ortolan jumped in. \'93We calculated how the fields received by the apparatus could have blurred the images.\'94\
\'93Received?\'94 the astrophysicist checked.\
\'93Generated\'94, Ortolan corrected. \'93By our apparatus,\'94 he stuttered. \'93Then we used that calculation to reverse that effect.\'94\
\'93And that worked?\'94\
\'93Not with the accepted value of G\'97the universe\'92s gravitational constant. When we repeated the calculation with a slightly higher value for G, the correction resolved the data we received\'97from the apparatus.\'94\
\'93What led you to vary G?\'94 the astrophysicist asked.\
\'93Components of\'97\'94 Ortolan stopped and restarted. \'93The apparatus interacts with gravity. I hypothesised we could tune the data\'97\'94\
\'93By varying a constant,\'94 a physicist challenged.\
Ortolan shrugged. \'93Either the data was out of focus, or it just coincidentally formed a pattern. Pattern recognition is essentially psychological phenomena. I wanted to eliminate psychology. Call me curious. I wanted to explore whether the data misalignment resulted from a signal originating under different gravitational conditions.\'94\
The cryptologist approached the display. \'93Can you show another section with the same correction?\'94\
Vilnius moved along the image. More markings resolved.\
\'93And another.\'94\
He moved again.\
\'93Those groups recur,\'94 the cryptologist said. \'93There, and there. Could you send me both versions?\'94\
\'93Already sent.\'94\
\'93Have you tried another sample?\'94 the astrophysicist asked.\
\'93Two so far,\'94 Ortolan said. \'93The correction that worked for this one made the other worse.\'94\
Vilnius brought up the second sample. \'93It required a lower value for G.\'94\
\'93So there is something out there sending coherent information tuned for different values for G?\'94 a physicist asked.\
\'93Or multiple somethings sending out information assuming their own constant for G,\'94 another said.\
\'93Or not knowing their G distorts their information,\'94 said another.\
\'93It all resolves to the same conclusion,\'94 Johnny said.\
The cryptologist shook her head. \'93If the corrections reflect conditions at the source, either G is different where the information originates or it\'92s being sent out targeting different values for G.\'94\
\'93Either that, or someone is galactically taxing with us,\'94 Johnny said.\
\'93G is universal,\'94 the astrophysicist said. \'93If it really differs at those sources, we\'92re talking about multiple universes.\'94\
\'93Universes who are trying to talk to us?\'94 Sarah asked. \
The cryptologist was examining the images on her tablet. \'93Whatever the explanation, this gives us more to work with.\'94\
Johnny stood. For a moment he seemed uncertain what to do once upright.\
\'93That\'92s\'85 good work, Doctor.\'94\
Ortolan waited.\
\'93Very good work.\'94\
Sarah watched Johnny lean across the table and offer his hand. Ortolan took it.\
\'93You\'92ve made my day.\'94\
\'93Thank you, Johnny.\'94\
The handshake stopped.\
\'93What about the other constants?\'94 Johnny asked. \'93Have you checked those?\'94\
\'93Not yet.\'94\
\'93So we shouldn\'92t get ahead of ourselves.\'94\
Ortolan withdrew his hand.\
\'93Can we process the remaining samples?\'94\
\'93Yes. We can divide them among our team.\'94\
\'93Good.\'94\
\'93And I\'92d like to see your calculations,\'94 the astrophysicist said.\
Ortolan nodded and reached for his tablet.\
The conservator pointed at the screen. \'93Could we see the wall? I mean, the actual location.\'94\
Johnny sat down. \'93You want to visit the lab?\'94\
\'93If there\'92s anything left of the surface, yes.\'94\
\'93We have fragments,\'94 Ortolan said.\
\'93I\'92d like to see those.\'94\
